rength and interference can vary. By intelligently modifying the transmission rate, the standard aims to maximize throughput and minimize packet loss, thereby improving the overall user experience. [Data: Entities (245, 278, 246, 279), Relationships (333, 366, 334, 367,+more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Frequency Bands and Channel Utilization\",\n      \"explanation\": \"IEEE 802.11 standards operate across different frequency bands, primarily the 2.4GHz and 5GHz bands, each offering distinct advantages in terms of range and throughput. 'BANDA_2.4GHZ_CANAIS' refers to the channels used within the 2.4GHz band, which is a widely adopted range for Wi-Fi due to its balance of range and penetration capabilities. The IEEE 802.11 standard defines how devices communicate over these channels, enabling wireless networking. Concurrently, 'BANDA_5GHZ_CANAIS' denotes the channels within the 5GHz band. This band typically offers a wider selection of channels and experiences less interference compared to the 2.4GHz band, which can be more crowded. The utilization of these specific channels within both bands is fundamental for managing interference and ensuring efficient data transfer, forming the basis for wireless local area networks (WLANs). [Data: Entities (280, 281), Relationships (368,+more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Collision Avoidance Mechanism: CSMA/CA\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The CSMA/CA (Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance) protocol is a fundamental component of Wi-Fi networks, specifically within the IEEE 802.11 standards, governing how devices access the shared wireless medium. This protocol is designed to prevent data collisions, which can occur when multiple devices attempt to transmit data simultaneously. Before transmitting, a device 'listens' to the medium to detect if it is currently in use. If the medium is free, the device transmits, but it also employs avoidance techniques to minimize the probability of a collis